%S A399016 111,212,221,313,331,414,422,441,515,551,616,623,632,661,717,771,818,
%T A399016 824,842,881,919,933,991,1025,1052,1226,1234,1243,1262,1427,1472,1535,
%U A399016 1553,1628,1644,1682,1829,1836,1863,1892,2045,2054,2137,2173,2438,2446,2464,2483,2555,2739
%N A399016 Numbers that can be obtained by concatenating the decimal representations of x, y, and x/y for x >= 1 and y a divisor of x.
%C A399016 The division analog of A398649, which concatenates x, y, and x+y; the divisibility condition y | x makes this sequence comparatively sparse.
%C A399016 The 23 three-digit terms d1 d2 d3 satisfy d1 = d2*d3 (the first digit is the product of the other two), since x = y*(x/y); they are those with 1 <= y <= x <= 9 and y dividing x.
%C A399016 A term may arise from more than one pair (x, y); the smallest such is 11111, from x = 11, y = 1 (11/1 = 11) and x = 11, y = 11 (11/11 = 1).
%F A399016 If y has q digits and x/y has r digits, then the term formed from (x, y) is x*10^(q + r) + y*10^r + x/y.
%e A399016 111 is a term because it is the concatenation of x = 1, y = 1 and x/y = 1.
%e A399016 212 is a term because it is the concatenation of x = 2, y = 1 and x/y = 2.
%e A399016 11111 is a term because it can be formed by x = 11, y = 1 or x = 11, y = 11.
%t A399016 f[x_, y_] := FromDigits[Join @@ IntegerDigits /@ {x, y, x/y}];
%t A399016 nn = 3000; Union@Reap[x = 1; While[FromDigits[Join @@ IntegerDigits /@ {x, 1, 1}] <= nn, Do[If[Divisible[x, y], v = f[x, y]; If[v <= nn, Sow[v]]], {y, x}]; x++]][[2, 1]]
%o A399016 (Python)
%o A399016 from sympy import divisors
%o A399016 def aupto(limit):
%o A399016     terms = set()
%o A399016     x = 1
%o A399016     while int(f"{x}11") <= limit:
%o A399016         for y in divisors(x):
%o A399016             v = int(f"{x}{y}{x // y}")
%o A399016             if v <= limit:
%o A399016                 terms.add(v)
%o A399016         x += 1
%o A399016     return sorted(terms)
%o A399016 print(aupto(3000))
%Y A399016 Cf. A398566, A398647, A398649.
%K A399016 nonn,base,new
%O A399016 1,1
